Old movies and office jokes often reflect sexist tropes and past workplace power dynamics, not a rule about real people. The “secretary and boss” storyline was common precisely because it was sensational, unequal, and easy to turn into gossip—not because assistants as a group behave that way.

In a real workplace, the relevant question is not whether a stereotype exists; it is whether there is specific, observable conduct that crosses professional boundaries. A manager should:

- treat the assistant like any other employee;
- avoid personal or sexualized comments, private favoritism, and reciprocal flirting;
- make work decisions transparently;
- address actual inappropriate behavior factually and consistently;
- involve HR if there is a reporting-line relationship and any mutual personal interest.

Assuming sexual motives based on her gender or job title is unfair and can itself contribute to a hostile workplace.
